---
/**
 * What GitHub Pages serves for an address that matches no file.
 *
 * There was no such page for a long time, which meant every mistyped or stale
 * URL landed on the host's own default: a grey page with no sidebar, no search
 * and no way back into the guide. The addresses the guide itself abandoned are
 * handled a level up, by the redirect table in `legacy-urls.mjs`, so anything
 * that reaches this page is a typo, a truncated link, or a page that never
 * existed. All three want the same thing, which is a route back in.
 *
 * The filename matters: Astro emits `src/pages/404.astro` as `dist/404.html`
 * rather than `dist/404/index.html`, and GitHub Pages looks for exactly that.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { guideHref } from "../nav";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Base
  title="Not found"
  seoTitle="Page not found"
  description="There is no page at this address. The guide index lists every chapter."
  crumbs={[{ label: "Not found" }]}
  noindex
>
  <article>
    <h1>Not found</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      There is no page at this address. No page was lost. The old addresses of
      the guide still work, and go to the chapter that replaced them. Thus a
      link that comes here has an error in the address, or the address is not
      complete.
    </p>

    <p>
      <a href={guideHref}>The guide index</a> lists every track, section and
      chapter in reading order. The index is the fastest way to find what you
      want. If you know the topic but not the page, use the search box in the
      header. The search box matches every chapter title and heading. Press
      <kbd>/</kbd> to open it.
    </p>

    <p>
      If you are new to the guide and do not look for one page, read{" "}
      <a href={`${base}paths/`}>the reading paths</a>. They give seven routes
      through the guide for different starting points, for example another
      systems language, a scripting language, or no systems experience.
    </p>

    <p>
      If a link on this site sent you here, that link is a bug. This should
      not be possible, because a browser test resolves every internal link on
      every page before a build can publish. In all cases,{" "}
      <a href={`${base}contact/`}>tell the author where you came from</a>, and the link
      will be fixed.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
